// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 2 (memory-audit-4e
// whole-branch review, probe P7): one LOCAL text pushed into TWO qualifying
// LOCAL lists. Originally (4e): both `p7L1` and `p7L2` kept their
// scope-exit element-free loops (only the pushed VALUE was disqualified),
// so both loops freed the one handle `p7X` shares with them: SIGSEGV. The
// 4e/pre-ARC fix disqualified BOTH receivers' DEEP release, independently,
// the moment each push saw a non-fresh (bare-identifier) value. Expected
// 4e/ARC-Task-4 leak count: 2 -- `p7X`'s text only (`p7X` itself was
// `lowFreeUnsafe`, handed to two push arguments, skipped entirely at scope
// exit; `p7L1`/`p7L2` themselves got only a shallow release each).
//
// ARC Task 5 (counted container element slots) closes this fixture to
// ZERO -- the two-receiver twin of p6_dup_push_local.cla's own updated
// comment (same mechanism, one shared value pushed into TWO containers
// instead of one container twice): both pushes now retain `p7X`'s value
// first (non-birth, bare identifier -- rc==3: `p7X`'s own +1 plus one
// retain per push); Task 5 deleted the non-fresh-push receiver consult, so
// BOTH `p7L1` and `p7L2` now qualify for a real DEEP scope-exit release
// (confirmed against the emitted C: an element-walk-then-release for each,
// rc 3 -> 2 -> 1); and `p7X`'s own push-argument handoff moved off
// `lowFreeUnsafe` onto shallow-safe (`lowEscapeWalkArgsSafeHandoff`), so
// `p7X` itself gets a real shallow release too (rc 1 -> 0). Three releases
// (two element-walks, one local scope-exit) exactly balance the three
// owners: no leak, no double-free.

func runP7TwoLists() {
    var p7L1: list of text
    var p7L2: list of text
    var p7X: text = "shared"
    p7L1.push(p7X)
    p7L2.push(p7X)
    alert("p7 done")
}
